-- title:
--   The V-branch of the crossing model is (W/π)[[T]]
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring and let $\pi \in W$. Write $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\,W\,\pi$ for the quotient of the two-variable power series ring $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}\,(\mathrm{Fin}\ 2)\,W$ by the ideal `uvCrossingIdeal W π` generated by the single element $X_0 X_1 - C(\pi)$, i.e. for $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$, and let `U π` and `V π` denote the two distinguished elements of this ring. The assertion is that there exists a ring isomorphism
--   $$e : \bigl(\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\,W\,\pi\bigr)\big/ (V\pi) \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; (W/(\pi))[[T]]$$
--   from the quotient of the crossing model by the principal ideal generated by `V π` onto the one-variable power series ring over $W/(\pi)$, subject to three normalisations: $e$ sends the class of `U π` to the variable $T$, it sends the class of `V π` to $0$, and for every $w \in W$ it sends the class of $\mathrm{const}\,\pi\,w$, the image in the crossing model of the constant power series $C(w)$, to the constant power series whose coefficient is the residue class of $w$ in $W/(\pi)$. The isomorphism is produced as an additive and multiplicative bijection; no hypotheses beyond commutativity of $W$ are imposed, and in particular $\pi$ is arbitrary.
--
--   This identifies one of the two branches of the local crossing model $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$: killing $v$ collapses the model to a smooth formal branch, a one-variable power series ring over $W/(\pi)$, the algebraic shadow of the two transversal components of the special fibre of the Deligne–Rapoport model of $X_0(p)$ at a supersingular point. It is the companion of the statement for the other branch, and is used by the local-ring results that read off branches, crossing presentations, and the height-one primes of a ring whose adic completion is a crossing model.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_UVCrossingModel_exists_ringEquiv_quotient_span_V_powerSeries.lean

import Mathlib
